    const SEG_YT = [[0, 'Why do we forget things we clearly learned? You sit through a lecture, take notes, feel like you get it — and a week later most of it is gone. That isn\'t a personal failing. It\'s one of the most reliable findings in psychology.', '为什么我们会忘记明明学过的东西？听完课、记了笔记、觉得自己懂了——一周后大部分却都忘了。这不是个人的问题，而是心理学里最可靠的发现之一。'], [30, 'In the 1880s Hermann Ebbinghaus decided to measure forgetting directly, and he tested himself on nonsense syllables for years — DAX, BOK, YAT — three letters with no meaning, so nothing he already knew could help.', '19 世纪 80 年代，艾宾浩斯决定直接测量遗忘。他用无意义音节——DAX、BOK、YAT——测试自己好几年。三个字母没有任何含义，已有知识帮不上忙。'], [60, 'He learned each list until he could recite it perfectly twice in a row, and recorded how long that took. Then he waited — twenty minutes, an hour, a day, a month — and learned the same list again.', '他把每张列表背到能连续两次完美复述，并记录所用时间。然后等待——二十分钟、一小时、一天、一个月——再重新学习同一张列表。'], [90, 'What he measured was savings: how much faster the second round was. If relearning took half as long, then in some sense half of the memory was still there.', '他测量的是“节省量”：第二次学习快了多少。如果重学只用一半时间，那么从某种意义上说，一半的记忆仍然保留着。'], [120, 'Thousands of lists, all on himself. Tedious work — but it produced the first clean curve of how memory fades over time.', '成千上万张列表，全部在自己身上实验。工作很枯燥，却第一次画出了记忆随时间衰退的清晰曲线。'], [150, 'And the shape of that curve is the surprising part. Forgetting isn\'t steady. It\'s steep at the start, and then it flattens out.', '曲线的形状才是最出人意料的地方。遗忘并不是匀速的：开头非常陡，之后逐渐变平。'], [180, 'Within twenty minutes he\'d already lost a large share of a list. After an hour, more than half. After a day, roughly two-thirds.', '二十分钟内，他就忘掉了一张列表的相当一部分；一小时后超过一半；一天后大约三分之二。'], [210, 'So most of the drop happens in the first day after learning. Whatever survives that first day tends to stick around for a long time.', '所以大部分遗忘发生在学习后的第一天。熬过第一天的内容，往往能保留很久。'], [240, 'Context matters too. Divers who learned words underwater remembered them better underwater than on the beach — the setting itself acts as a cue for recall.', '情境也很重要。在水下学单词的潜水员，在水下回忆得比在海滩上更好——环境本身就是回忆的线索。'], [270, 'The good news: spacing out reviews flattens the forgetting curve. Each review, timed just as you start to forget, makes the next drop shallower.', '好消息是：间隔复习能让遗忘曲线变平。每次在快要忘记时复习，下一次的下降就会更缓。']];
    const SEG_VID = [[0, 'Okay, let\'s get started. Last week we built the three-store model. Today is the other side of it — forgetting — and why it happens even when you did everything right.', '好，我们开始。上周我们讲了三存储模型，今天讲它的另一面——遗忘，以及为什么即使你都做对了也还是会忘。'], [30, 'There are three places memory can fail: at encoding, in storage, and at retrieval. Most of what feels like forgetting is actually the third one.', '记忆可能在三个环节出问题：编码、存储和提取。大多数“忘了”的感觉，其实属于第三种。'], [60, 'Encoding failure is the simplest. You can\'t forget what never got in. Quick test — what\'s on the back of a one-dollar bill? Most of you have handled thousands.', '编码失败最简单：没进来的东西谈不上忘。快速测试一下——一美元纸币背面印的是什么？你们大多数人摸过成千上万张。'], [90, 'Storage decay is the idea that traces fade with time. It\'s real, but it\'s slower than people assume, and it\'s hard to separate from interference.', '存储衰退指记忆痕迹会随时间变淡。它确实存在，但比大家以为的慢，而且很难和干扰区分开。'], [120, 'Interference comes in two directions. Proactive: old learning gets in the way of new. Retroactive: new learning disrupts recall of the old. Write both down — they\'re on the exam.', '干扰有两个方向。前摄干扰：旧的学习妨碍新的学习；倒摄干扰：新的学习妨碍对旧内容的回忆。两个都记下来——考试会考。'], [150, 'And retrieval failure — the memory is there, you just can\'t reach it. Tip-of-the-tongue is the classic example. The fix is better cues, not more repetition.', '还有提取失败——记忆还在，只是够不到。“话到嘴边说不出”就是经典例子。解决办法是更好的线索，而不是更多重复。']];
    const SEG_AUD = [[0, 'Sam (TA)', 'Okay, a lot of you asked about the difference between encoding and storage, so let\'s start there. Encoding is getting it in. Storage is keeping it.', '好，很多同学问编码和存储有什么区别，我们就从这里开始。编码是“放进去”，存储是“留住它”。'], [30, 'Student', 'Is storage just short-term memory, then? Like, the thing that lasts twenty seconds?', '那存储是不是就是短时记忆？就是只能保持二十秒的那个？'], [60, 'Sam (TA)', 'Not quite. Short-term memory is one store. Storage is the process across all of them — sensory, short-term and long-term.', '不完全是。短时记忆只是其中一个存储系统，而“存储”是贯穿感觉记忆、短时记忆和长时记忆的整个过程。'], [90, 'Student', 'So for the exam, if a question says the information was never processed for meaning, that\'s encoding?', '那考试时，如果题目说信息从没按含义处理过，那就是编码问题？'], [120, 'Sam (TA)', 'Exactly — that\'s shallow encoding. Look at Table 7.1 again: semantic is deepest, then acoustic, then visual. That order shows up a lot.', '没错——那是浅层编码。再看一下表 7.1：语义最深，其次是声音，再是视觉。这个顺序经常考。'], [150, 'Student', 'Got it. And retrieval is just… remembering it later?', '明白了。那提取就是……之后把它想起来？']];
    const OCR_TX = [['Sensory → STM → LTM', '感觉记忆 → 短时记忆 → 长时记忆'], ['attention ⟶ rehearsal ⟶', '注意 ⟶ 复述 ⟶'], ['STM ≈ 7 ± 2 items, ~20 s', '短时记忆 ≈ 7 ± 2 项，约 20 秒'], ['Exam: semantic > acoustic > visual', '考点：语义 > 声音 > 视觉']];
